```console
$ caliper-docgen extract --help
Extract prop types and write to stdout or --out file

Usage: caliper-docgen extract [OPTIONS]

Options:
  -s, --src <SRC>
          Source directories to scan [default: ./src]

  -o, --out <OUT>
          Output file [default: stdout]

  -f, --format <FORMAT>
          Output format

          Possible values:
          - canonical: caliper-docgen canonical schema (richest)
          - rdt:       react-docgen-typescript compatible output
          - toon:      Token-Optimized Object Notation (compact agent format)
          
          [default: canonical]

      --cache-dir <CACHE_DIR>
          Cache directory

      --no-cache
          Neither read nor write the on-disk caches (the declaration parse cache
          and the dependency index); the output is the same, a run just parses
          everything

      --html-attributes <HTML_ATTRIBUTES>
          How much of an inherited HTML element's attributes to expose

          Possible values:
          - curated: A compact subset per element: handlers, aria-*, ref and the
            common attributes
          - full:    All of `@types/react`'s real attributes for the element
            (matches RDT) (default)
          - none:    No inherited HTML attributes at all — own props only

      --extra-builtins <EXTRA_BUILTINS>
          Extra type names to treat as recognized/builtin (no "unknown type"
          warning) — e.g. a library-specific type this tool doesn't already know

  -v, --verbose...
          Verbose output (repeat for more: -v, -vv)

      --include-unexported
          Keep components their module doesn't export in `--format rdt`, which
          omits them by default as react-docgen-typescript does. Canonical
          output always keeps them, marked `exported: false`

  -q, --quiet
          Suppress all non-error output

      --config <CONFIG>
          Path to docgen.config.ts

  -j, --json
          Machine-readable JSON output: the canonical schema, compact, always to
          stdout, ignoring --out/--format (which govern the human/RDT/TOON paths
          below), and suppresses the human-readable summary/diagnostics

      --load-dependencies
          Load the type declarations props reference from installed packages (on
          by default; this flag turns it back on over a config that sets
          `loadDependencies: false`)

      --no-load-dependencies
          Do not load the type declarations props reference from installed
          packages

      --collapse-inherited-props <COLLAPSE_INHERITED_PROPS>
          Which inherited sources to list as one inherited layer of names
          instead of prop by prop: large ambient ones (DOM and ARIA attributes,
          style systems) from loaded dependencies by default; a dependency's own
          API is listed unless it declares more than 200 props

          Possible values:
          - dependencies: Large ambient sources (DOM and ARIA attributes, style
            systems) from loaded dependencies (default)
          - all:          Those, and project sources of more than 30 props
          - none:         List every prop

      --experimental-semantic-types
          Experimental: ask the built-in TypeScript 7.1 checker (`tsrs`) for
          every component's props in one program and wait for the answer, which
          replaces the structural one prop by prop; the structural result stands
          wherever the checker cannot answer (off by default)

      --tsc-path <TSC_PATH>
          A checker binary for `--experimental-semantic-types` to run instead of
          the built-in one: `typescript@7.1`'s `tsc`, or `tsrs`

      --semantic-timeout-ms <SEMANTIC_TIMEOUT_MS>
          With `--experimental-semantic-types`: how long the checker may take to
          start or to answer any one request, in milliseconds, before the run
          takes the structural result with one warning
          
          [default: 10000]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

```
